# Well-Architected — always on

Canonical hub: [AWS Well-Architected](https://aws.amazon.com/architecture/well-architected/). Eventsliner map: [docs/21-platform-standards.md](docs/21-platform-standards.md).

Applies **regardless of host** (Render, Vercel, AWS). Do not require AWS services to follow these pillars.

## MUST (by pillar)

| Pillar | Bar for Eventsliner |
|--------|---------------------|
| **Operational excellence** | Structured request logs; `GET /health`; documented deploy; reversible migrations |
| **Security** | AuthZ via `can()` / API key scopes; CSRF on session mutations; HMAC webhooks; security headers; secrets only in env |
| **Reliability** | Managed Postgres; payment webhook reconciliation; graceful Redis/email fallbacks |
| **Performance** | Prefer India-proximate regions; paginate lists; avoid N+1 in new queries |
| **Cost** | Modular monolith; no new microservices/queues unless a phase doc requires them |
| **Sustainability** | Efficient pagination; no chatty polling without backoff |

## MUST NOT

- Ship features that skip health/logging/authz “for speed.”
- Store production PII only on ephemeral disk.
- Hard-code secrets or commit `.env`.
- Add Kubernetes/Kafka/service mesh for v1 taste.

## When designing a change

Ask: How do we observe it? How do we secure it? How does it fail? What does it cost? Document answers in the PR/commit body for non-trivial work.
